---
import Button from "~/components/Button.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Stacking many small edges"
  description="Combining useful strategies can improve a portfolio. The gains depend on their size, shared risks, costs and what you sell to buy them."
>
  <Hero
    title="Can you beat the market by stacking many small edges?"
    lede="It can help. But a fund count and a win rate cannot tell you whether the combined portfolio is better. You need to know how much each idea gains or loses, when it fails, and what pays for it."
  >
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2">
      <Verdict word="Too close to call" gloss="Useful diversification is possible; the advantage of a particular combination has to be tested." />
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2 id="the-arithmetic">A 55% win rate is only a start</h2>
    <p>An idea can win slightly more often than it loses and still lose money overall if its bad outcomes are
    much larger. The same applies to beating an index: the size of the lead and the size of the shortfall matter.
    A backtest's win rate is also an estimate, not a known probability for the future.</p>
    <p>Combining ideas with positive expected returns can make the outcome more dependable when their losses
    do not arrive together. That is the useful part of the stacking idea. It does not require a huge number of
    funds, and adding another fund does not guarantee another source of return.</p>

    <h2 id="why-it-stops">Shared risks limit the benefit</h2>
    <p>Two value funds may own different companies but suffer when investors avoid cheap stocks. Two trend
    funds may trade many of the same markets. Different names and different managers can still leave you with
    much the same bet.</p>
    <p>A model that gives every idea the same expected gain, the same risk and the same positive correlation
    produces a ceiling on the benefit of adding more. That ceiling belongs to those assumptions. It does not
    establish a maximum chance of success for every portfolio, or rule out a new strategy with different risks.</p>

    <h2 id="how-many-bets">Look for different reasons to earn a return</h2>
    <p>Broad stocks, cheaper companies, momentum, trend following and government bonds have different roles,
    but they can overlap. Geography can diversify company, country and currency exposure even when markets
    fall together. The useful question is how much a holding changes the whole portfolio, especially during
    the kinds of loss you need to withstand.</p>
    <p>A hedge can be useful even if it trails stocks on its own. It may reduce a deep loss or provide money
    to rebalance. That benefit must be weighed against what the hedge costs and the return of the investment
    sold to buy it. See <a href="/strategies/crash-protection/">crash protection</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="the-four-portfolios">What pays for the new idea?</h2>
    <p>Selling stocks to buy a strategy gives up some stock return. Selling bonds changes a different part of
    the portfolio. Borrowing inside a fund can preserve the stock allocation, but adds financing costs and
    extra market exposure. These are different choices and need different comparisons.</p>
    <p>A fully funded mix can improve compounded growth by reducing the damage from volatility. It does not
    need borrowing to be useful. Nor does financing turn an uncertain strategy into free extra return.
    We compare both the growth and the losses of the resulting portfolio after costs.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/portfolios/value-lean/#fund-alternatives">fund comparison</a> shows how replacing a holding
    changes the entire Value lean portfolio. Its results apply to those funds and that period. They are not
    independent gains that can simply be added to a trend or bond result.</p>

    <h2 id="how-long">How to judge the evidence</h2>
    <p>More observations can improve an estimate. They cannot guarantee that a strategy will keep working,
    or that a simulated index can be bought at the assumed cost. Evidence from different markets, periods and
    implementations helps distinguish a durable mechanism from a fortunate sample.</p>
    <p>Uncertainty does not require holding nothing until a result becomes statistically clear. It calls for
    a weight you can live with if the idea disappoints, an explicit alternative, and a reason to revisit the choice.</p>

    <h2 id="more-funds">Each addition should earn its place</h2>
    <p>Keep an addition when it improves a goal that matters to you after accounting for costs, shared risks
    and the investment it replaces. Remove it when a simpler holding serves that goal as well. There is no
    universal best number of funds.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href="/portfolios/">Compare the portfolios</Button>
    <Button href="/strategies/" variant="outline">Other strategies</Button>
  </div>
</Base>
